You do not need Custom_Application for the LCD or keypad. In your EuroBEEB OS dump the Jobber drivers are already present in the OS ROM:

Viewline 4.00 — LCD driver
CU-key 99 4.00 — keyboard driver
LCD init routine at &C14F
reset/service-3 startup already calls that init routine
The custom app just selects the local LCD/keyboard channels and then uses ordinary PRINT/GET. The relevant lines are effectively:

*FX3,0
*FX2,0
VDU 30
PRINT TAB(0,0);"..."
R=GET
Minimal standalone setup
Type this into BBC BASIC IV with no application ROM running:

10 REM Standalone Viewline LCD + CU-key keyboard test
20 *FX3,0
30 *FX2,0
40 CALL &C14F
50 VDU 30
60 PRINT TAB(0,0);"LCD OK"
70 PRINT TAB(0,1);"PRESS A KEY:"
80 REPEAT
90   K=GET
100 UNTIL K<>0
110 PRINT TAB(12,1);CHR$(K)
120 GOTO 80
CALL &C14F is optional after a clean reset — the OS already calls it — but it is safe and useful if the display was left in an odd state by another program.

What the *FX values do here
On this machine, the important ones are:

Purpose	Commands
Local LCD display + local keypad	*FX3,0 and *FX2,0
Serial terminal only	*FX3,7 and *FX2,1
Serial connected, but GET still reads local keys	*FX2,2
That matches both the custom app and the EURO terminal source:

*FX3,0 — output to the local VDU/LCD path
*FX3,7 — output to serial only
*FX2,0 — local keyboard input
*FX2,1 — serial input
*FX2,2 — serial enabled, keyboard still read
So after any serial/downloading session, always set:

*FX3,0
*FX2,0
otherwise PRINT may go to the serial port and appear to do nothing on the LCD.

Using the display
The Viewline is 24 columns × 2 rows, so use:

PRINT TAB(0,0);"LINE ONE"
PRINT TAB(0,1);"LINE TWO"
Useful control codes supported by the OS driver include:

VDU 12 — clear display
VDU 30 — home / reset cursor
VDU 8, VDU 9 — cursor left/right
VDU 10, VDU 11 — cursor down/up
VDU 13 — carriage return
VDU 23,... — user-defined character support
What you can ignore from the custom app
These are not needed for basic LCD/keyboard use:

BASE.=&FE00
TURNOFF 8:OUTCH 8
?&FE13=&18
?&FE0C=&0C
?&FE12=&09
Those are for general VIA/ACIA/control I/O used by the application. The OS already owns the LCD and keyboard hardware through its own driver routines.

Control BASIC note
You can still use Control BASIC keywords such as DELAY, CLOCK$, DATE$, SAMPLE, etc. Just avoid driving the same VIA lines that the OS peripheral drivers use unless you know exactly which port lines are free.

In short: boot to BASIC, set *FX3,0 and *FX2,0, optionally call &C14F, then use PRINT, GET, GET$ and INPUT normally.
